<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Library of Nineveh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Library of Ashurbanipal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nineveh">Nineveh</a>, also known as the Library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashurbanipal">Ashurbanipal</a>, was a collection of thousands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> tablets assembled by the Assyrian king Ashurbanipal in the seventh century BCE. It contained literary, religious, scholarly and administrative texts, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epic-of-gilgamesh">Epic of Gilgamesh</a>. Its rediscovery in the nineteenth century was fundamental to modern knowledge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assyrian-empire">Assyrian Empire</a>.</p></div>
